## 1. Checking Out a Branch
Command
git checkout main


What it does:
Switches your working directory to the main branch.

2. Viewing Commit History
Command
git log


What you saw:
Two commits:

Main branch commit

Initial commit

3. Viewing What a Commit Changed
Command
git show <commit-id>


Example:

git show b2c80cd2fc3d078aedec4411191ba742addb4c13


What it does:
Shows:

Commit metadata (author, date, message)

The exact file changes (diff)

4. Viewing Only File Names Changed in a Commit
Command
git show --name-only <commit-id>


Example:

git show --name-only b2c80cd2f...


Output:

newFileMain.txt
stagedFile.txt
unstagedFile.txt

5. Understanding Why ls Failed

You typed:

ls


Windows CMD doesn't support ls.

Use:

dir

6. Switching Between Branches
Commands
git checkout FeatureBranch
git checkout main


What it does:
Changes the branch & updates the working directory to that branch's version of files.

7. Commit Comparison Between Branches

You used:

git log
git show --name-only <commit-id>


This helped you see:

FeatureBranch has 2 commits

main has only 2 commits

Both share the Initial commit

This demonstrates branch divergence.

8. Viewing Branch Movement History (Super Important)
Command
git reflog


What it does:
Shows EVERY movement of HEAD, including:

checkouts

commits

resets

Even if you lose commits, reflog can recover them.

Example lines:

HEAD@{3}: commit: Second commit in feature branch
HEAD@{8}: commit: Main branch commit
HEAD@{9}: commit (initial): Initial commit

9. Checking Help for git show
Command
git show -h


Purpose:
Shows available options instead of doing an operation.

10. Pushing to GitHub (First Time Push of main)

When you ran:

git push


You got:

fatal: The current branch main has no upstream branch.


So Git suggested the fix:

git push --set-upstream origin main


What this does:

Pushes the branch to GitHub

Connects local main ↔ remote origin/main

Future git push works without extra flags

11. Cloning the Repository Again into a New Folder

You ran:

git clone https://github.com/... sample123   # repo already existed


Error:

destination path already exists


So you did:

git clone https://github.com/... newRepoSitory


This created a fresh copy of the same GitHub repo.

12. Adding a New File & Committing in the New Repo

Commands:

echo testCommit > testCommit.txt
git add .
git commit -m "test commit"


What you did here:
Created a new commit in the freshly cloned repo.

13. Pushing the New Commit
Command
git push


Since the fresh repo already had upstream tracking, push succeeded.

14. Pulling That Commit Into the Original Repo

Back in your original folder:

git pull


This fetched the new commit and did a fast-forward merge.

Output showed:

Updating b2c80cd..743a59a
Fast-forward
 testCommit.txt | 1 +


What happened:
Your original repo is now up-to-date with GitHub.

15. Final Log After Pull

You checked:

git log


And saw:

test commit (from newRepoSitory)

Main branch commit

Initial commit

✔ Everything is synced perfectly.

